{0 The workero CLI}

[workero] turns an OCaml executable with [(modes wasm)] into a directory that
Wrangler can serve and deploy. [workero build] runs dune first.
[workero package] works on output that dune already built.

{1 Usage}

{@sh[
workero build [<dune build args>...] <dir>/<name>.bc.wasm.js [--out <dir>] [--allow-missing-primitives]
workero package <path>/<name>.bc.wasm.js [--out <dir>] [--allow-missing-primitives]
]}

[workero] has two options of its own, and both commands take them:

- [--out <dir>] (or [--out=<dir>]) sets the output directory. The default is
  [_worker].
- [--allow-missing-primitives] turns the missing-primitives error into a
  warning.

They can be anywhere in the arguments.

{2 build}

[workero build] removes its own options from the arguments and passes every
other argument to [dune build] in the same order. It adds [--profile release]
unless another profile is selected. There is no [--] separator.

{@sh[
workero build src/main.bc.wasm.js --profile dev --display short
]}

Exactly one argument must end in [.bc.wasm.js]. It is the target to package,
written as you would write it for [dune build]. Without an explicit dune root,
write it relative to the current directory. With none, or with more than one,
[workero] prints a usage error and exits with code 1. Other targets and aliases
in the arguments go to dune, but [workero] does not package them.

Run it from any directory inside the dune project.

[workero build] adds [--profile release] unless the arguments select a profile
with [--profile], [--release], [-p] or [--for-release-of-packages], or
[DUNE_PROFILE] is set.

{2 package}

[workero package _build/default/src/main.bc.wasm.js] reads
[_build/default/src/main.bc.wasm.js] and
[_build/default/src/main.bc.wasm.assets] and never runs dune. Use it when
another step builds the executable, such as a Makefile or CI job. A path that
does not end in [.bc.wasm.js] is a usage error.

{1 Where it finds the build output}

[workero build] finds the output of the [.bc.wasm.js] target the way dune
does, so it works when the project is inside another dune project, when you
run it from a subdirectory, and when you move the build directory.

+ The dune root is [--root] from the arguments, if set. With [--release],
  [-p], or [--for-release-of-packages] and no [--root], it is the current
  directory. Otherwise [DUNE_ROOT], if set, supplies the root. If none of these
  selects a root,
  it is the outermost parent directory of the current directory that has a
  [dune-workspace] file. If there is none, it is the outermost parent that has
  a [dune-project] file. If there is none, it is the current directory.
+ The build directory is [--build-dir] from the arguments, or
  [DUNE_BUILD_DIR], or [_build]. A relative path is relative to the dune root.
+ The output is in [<build directory>/default/<path of the target from the
  root>].

With an explicit root ([--root], [DUNE_ROOT], [--release], [-p], or
[--for-release-of-packages]),
[workero build] locates the target relative to that root, not to the current
directory. The output is in the directory of the target, and the name is the
file name without [.bc.wasm.js].

{1 What it does}

+ [workero build] only: builds the loader with dune.
+ Removes the [*.wasm.assets] directories that earlier runs left in the output
  directory, so a second run leaves one set of [.wasm] files.
+ Copies the wasm_of_ocaml loader ([<name>.bc.wasm.js]) and its
  [<name>.bc.wasm.assets] directory into the output directory.
+ Writes [load-wasm.js], which loads the precompiled wasm modules in workerd.
+ Writes an initial [index.js] and runs the program once in [workerd test] to
  read class names registered at module initialization with
  {!Workero.Durable_object.register}, {!Workero.Entrypoint.register} and {!Workero.Workflow.register}. The probe runs for at most 30 seconds by
  default. Set [WORKERO_PROBE_TIMEOUT] to a positive number of seconds to
  change it; absent, invalid and nonpositive values use 30 seconds. The probe
  does not call a fetch, scheduled, queue or email handler.
+ Writes the final [index.js]. It imports every [.wasm] file and [connect] from
  [cloudflare:sockets] (see {{!page-socket}Sockets}), exports the registered
  {!Workero.serve}, {!Workero.scheduled}, {!Workero.queue} and
  {!Workero.email} handlers through its default export, and adds named exports
  for registered Durable Object and entrypoint classes. If Durable Object
  classes exist, it writes [durable-object.js] for their initialization, fetch,
  alarm, and WebSocket event handlers. If entrypoint classes exist, it writes
  [entrypoint.js] for their RPC methods (see {{!page-entrypoints}Entrypoints}).
  If Workflow classes exist, it writes [workflow.js], which imports
  [cloudflare:workflows] (see {{!page-workflows}Workflows}). It removes a stale
  [durable-object.js], [entrypoint.js] or [workflow.js] when no classes of that
  kind are registered.

On success it prints [workero: wrote <dir> (<n> wasm file(s))] and, Durable
Object classes were found, [workero: Durable Object classes: <names>] and, if
entrypoint classes were found, [workero: entrypoint classes: <names>] and, if
Workflow classes were found, [workero: workflow classes: <names>]. Its explicit
usage and packaging errors print [workero: <reason>] to stderr and exit with
code 1. Other errors, such as file-system exceptions during a copy, are not
wrapped by that error handler.

A build with no registered fetch, scheduled, queue, email or Durable Object handler
can complete, but its generated worker fails to load with [registered no
handler]. Register at least one handler at module initialization.

{1 Errors}

[workero build] and [workero package] use [workero: <reason>] and exit with
code 1 for these handled errors:

- the arguments do not match the usage,
- [dune build] fails ([workero build] only),
- dune does not produce the loader or the [.wasm.assets] directory (the
  executable lacks [(modes wasm)], or the path is wrong),
- the target is outside the dune root ([workero build] only),
- [workerd] cannot be found on [PATH], through [WORKERD] or in a parent
  [node_modules/.bin] directory. If [WORKERD] is set to a nonempty value, it
  takes precedence; a name without a slash is looked up on [PATH], while a
  path is checked directly,
- the program fails during initialization in [workerd]. Its [workerd] output
  is printed before [workero: the program failed to initialise in workerd],
- initialization takes longer than [WORKERO_PROBE_TIMEOUT] (30 seconds by
  default). [workero] kills the probe and prints its output and the timeout,
- [workerd] exits successfully but does not report registered class names,
- the program uses OCaml functions that have no wasm implementation. The
  message is [workero: <name> uses functions with no wasm implementation:
  <list>]. They fail at run time if called. Provide a wasm implementation
  with [(wasm_of_ocaml (wasm_files ...))], drop the library that needs them,
  or pass [--allow-missing-primitives] if they are never called.

The check also works for programs built with [--pretty] in [wasm_of_ocaml]
[flags].

{1 With Wrangler}

Point [main] at the generated [index.js] and run [workero build] as the build
command, so [wrangler dev] and [wrangler deploy] rebuild the worker:

{@toml[
main = "_worker/index.js"

[build]
command = "workero build src/main.bc.wasm.js"
watch_dir = "src"
]}

wasm_of_ocaml needs [wasm-opt] from binaryen 119 or later on [PATH]. The npm
package [binaryen] provides it.

{1 Advanced: a dune rule}

To let dune own the whole build, call [workero package] from a rule
in the [dune] file next to the executable:

{@dune[
(executable
 (name main)
 (modes wasm)
 (libraries workero))

(rule
 (targets (dir worker))
 (deps main.bc.wasm.js main.bc.wasm.assets)
 (action
  (run %{bin:workero} package main.bc.wasm.js --out worker)))
]}

[dune build --profile release src/worker] builds the executable and writes the
worker to [_build/default/src/worker].

Point Wrangler at the built directory:

{@toml[
main = "_build/default/src/worker/index.js"

[build]
command = "dune build --profile release src/worker"
watch_dir = "src"
]}

[(targets (dir ...))] is a directory target. Dune 3.24 and later support it
directly. Older versions need [(using directory-targets 0.1)] in
[dune-project].
